  {
   "type": "culture",
   "title": "The bazaar: where everything has a name",
   "md": "The best place to practise *in chist?* is a bazaar. Every Iranian city has one – Tehran's [[بازار بزرگ|bāzār-e bozorg|Grand Bazaar]] is a roofed town of its own, with lanes for copper, carpets, spices, gold and paper. Shopkeepers are used to foreigners pointing and asking; many will tell you the word, make you repeat it, and offer tea.\n\nThe English word *bazaar* is itself Persian ([[بازار|bāzār]]), one of a long list of words that travelled west – you will meet the others in the advanced course.",
   "image": "tabriz-bazaar"
  },
  {
   "type": "tips",
   "title": "Watch out",
   "items": [
    "**در** is two words in one spelling: *dar* 'in' and *dar* 'door'. Context always tells you which: *dar kelās* (in the class) vs *dar-e kelās* (the class door).",
    "Learn each noun with its ezafe habit: after a final **-e** (خانه) or **-ā/-u/-i** the link sounds *-ye* and is written ی.",
    "Spoken Persian turns **ān** into **un** everywhere: *un, unjā, un-hā*. Written Persian keeps **ān**.",
    "Don't translate *the*. Say the bare noun and let context do the work."
   ]
